.TH "PROCESSINGINSTRUCTION" "3JS" "August 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
ProcessingInstruction \- ProcessingInstruction
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]ProcessingInstruction\f[B]\f[R] interface represents a \c
.UR https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#sec-pi
processing instruction
.UE \c
\ \(em a \f[CR]Node\f[R] that embeds an instruction targeting a specific
application, which can be ignored by any application that doesn\(cqt
recognize the instruction.
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.ProcessingInstruction()\f[R]
Creates a new ProcessingInstruction object instance.
.RS
.PP
Developers cannot use the \f[CR]ProcessingInstruction()\f[R] constructor
directly to create a new \f[CR]ProcessingInstruction\f[R] instance;
doing so results in an \(lqillegal constructor\(rq error.
Instead, use the \f[CR]document.createProcessingInstruction()\f[R]
method.
.RE
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
\f[I]This interface also inherits properties from its parent interfaces,
\f[CI]CharacterData\f[I], \f[CI]Node\f[I], and
\f[CI]EventTarget\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.sheet\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns the associated \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R] object, if any; or
\f[CR]null\f[R] if none.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.target\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
A name identifying the application to which the instruction is targeted.
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]This interface also inherits methods from its parent interfaces,
\f[CI]CharacterData\f[I], \f[CI]Node\f[I], and
\f[CI]EventTarget\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.getAttribute()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Retrieves the value of the named attribute from the current node and
returns it as a string.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.getAttributeNames()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns an array of attribute names from the current node.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.hasAttribute()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns a boolean value indicating if the element has the specified
attribute or not.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.hasAttributes()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns a boolean value indicating if the element has one or more HTML
attributes present.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.removeAttribute()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Removes the named attribute from the current node.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.setAttribute()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Sets the named attribute of the current node to a new value.
.TP
\f[B]ProcessingInstruction.toggleAttribute()\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Toggles a boolean attribute, removing it if it is present and adding it
if it is not present, on the specified element.
.PP
These methods provide easier access to the \f[CR]data\f[R] string
attributes.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Processing instructions, as the name suggests, specify how to process a
document.
They can include stylesheets for XML documents, placeholders for HTML
documents, or other processing instructions.
.PP
Processing instructions are \f[CR]Nodes\f[R] rather than
\f[CR]Elements\f[R].
They don\(cqt have children or cause nesting (as demonstrated in our
Patching example, and therefore don\(cqt change the shape of the
Document Object Model (DOM).
.PP
Initially, \f[CR]ProcessingInstruction\f[R] nodes were only supported in
XML documents, not HTML documents.
In non\-supporting browsers, processing instructions will be interpreted
as comments and represented as \f[CR]Comment\f[R] objects in the DOM
tree.
.PP
When written in documents directly, rather than created by
\f[CR]document.createProcessingInstruction()\f[R], they begin and end
with \f[CR]<?\f[R] and \f[CR]?>\f[R] delimiters, and contain a
\f[CR]target\f[R] and optional \f[CR]data\f[R] attributes.
For example:
.IP
.EX
<?my\-target name=\(dqmy\-name\(dq?>
.EE
.PP
When written in HTML, processing instructions can be provided with or
without the trailing \f[CR]?\f[R], and the browser will add it if not
supplied when parsing the DOM.
Both \f[CR]<?my\-target?>\f[R] and \f[CR]<?my\-target>\f[R] are
therefore valid.
XML is stricter and requires the trailing \f[CR]?\f[R].
.PP
HTML also has \c
.UR https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#processing-instruction-target-state
more restrictions on the \f[CR]target\f[R] name
.UE \c
\ in the HTML parser for backwards compatibility.
Effectively, it must match \f[CR][A\-Za\-z_][\-_A\-Za\-z0\-9]*\f[R] or
it is processed as a comment.
.PP
Although the syntax is identical to that of processing instructions, the
XML declaration (\f[CR]<?xml version=\(dq1.0\(dq?>\f[R]) is not
considered to be a processing instruction and is not added to the DOM.
.PP
User\-defined processing instructions cannot begin with
\f[CR]\(dqxml\(dq\f[R], as \f[CR]xml\f[R]\-prefixed
processing\-instruction target names are reserved by the XML
specification for particular, standard uses (for example,
\f[CR]<?xml\-stylesheet ?>\f[R]).
.PP
For backwards compatibility, if the target is \f[CR]xml\f[R] or
\f[CR]xml\-stylesheet\f[R], the processing instruction is parsed as a
comment in an HTML document.
This applies whether it is included in the original HTML or inserted
using a method such as \f[CR]Element.innerHTML\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
This example shows a processing instruction with a \f[CR]target\f[R] of
\f[CR]display\f[R] and \f[CR]data\f[R] of \f[CR]table\-view\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<?display table\-view?>
.EE
.SS Reserved XML target example
.IP
.EX
<?xml\-stylesheet href=\(dqstyles.css\(dq?>
.EE
.PP
This example shows a processing instruction with a target of
\f[CR]xml\-stylesheet\f[R] and \f[CR]data\f[R] of
\f[CR]href=\(dqstyles.css\(dq\f[R].
.SS Usage with \f[CR]<template for>\f[R] patching
This example uses the \f[CR]<?start>\f[R] and \f[CR]<?end>\f[R]
processing instructions as placeholders and later on fills in the
contents using \f[CR]<template for>\f[R].
Both exclude the optional trailing \f[CR]?\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<body>
  <div>
    <?start name=\(dqplaceholder\(dq>
    Loading...
    <?end>
  </div>
  ...
  <template for=\(dqplaceholder\(dq>
    Lorem Ipsum...
  </template>
  ...
</body>
.EE
.PP
This example also demonstrates the lack of processing instruction
children and nesting.
The \f[CR]<?start>\f[R] and \f[CR]<?end>\f[R] processing instructions,
although linked in terms of \f[CR]<template for>\f[R], are not linked in
terms of the DOM and do not cause the \f[CR]Loading...\f[R] content in
between to be a child (as demonstrated by the lack of indentation).
.SS Using methods as opposed to the \f[CR]data\f[R] attribute
This example creates a processing instruction using the
\f[CR]createProcessingInstruction()\f[R] method.
It then logs the processing instruction\(cqs data (accessed via its
\f[CR]CharacterData.data\f[R] property) and then its two attributes
individually (accessed via its
\f[CR]ProcessingInstruction.getAttribute()\f[R] method).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] pi = document.createProcessingInstruction(
  \(dqmy\-target\(dq,
  \(dqmy\-data1=\(aqvalue1\(aq my\-data2=\(aqvalue2\(aq\(dq,
);

console.log(pi.data);
console.log(pi.getAttribute(\(dqmy\-data1\(dq));
console.log(pi.getAttribute(\(dqmy\-data2\(dq));
\f[I]// logs\f[R]
\f[I]// my\-data1=\(aqvalue1\(aq my\-data2=\(aqvalue2\(aq\f[R]
\f[I]// value1\f[R]
\f[I]// value2\f[R]
.EE
